1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,613
A robot arm with every motor in the base.

2
00:00:02,613 --> 00:00:07,360
The joints are driven by rods and linkages, and the gripper stays level in every pose.

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:10,789
Laying out those linkages by hand is painful:

4
00:00:10,789 --> 00:00:14,428
link lengths, dead points, parts that crash into each other.

5
00:00:15,128 --> 00:00:19,921
Today I'll show you how to design one by simply describing it, and then watch it move.

6
00:00:19,921 --> 00:00:21,258
No CAD knowledge needed.

7
00:00:22,761 --> 00:00:23,507
We use SOMA.

8
00:00:23,507 --> 00:00:27,611
It's free and open source, and you can download it at somacad.app.

9
00:00:29,061 --> 00:00:34,781
We start a new model, and on the right we open Claude Code, our AI agent, right inside the model folder.

10
00:00:36,546 --> 00:00:39,197
Then I describe the arm in plain words:

11
00:00:39,197 --> 00:00:51,636
a desktop arm like a Dobot or uArm, all motors in the base, NEMA 17 steppers, push rods to the elbow, a parallelogram that keeps the gripper level, and about 250 millimeters of reach.

12
00:00:52,821 --> 00:00:54,719
Before it starts, it asks.

13
00:00:54,719 --> 00:00:55,813
A torque check:

14
00:00:55,813 --> 00:01:03,331
200 grams at 250 millimeters needs about 0.9 newton meters at the shoulder, twice what a NEMA 17 gives.

15
00:01:03,331 --> 00:01:06,980
So it suggests a GT2 belt with a 1 to 5 reduction.

16
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:08,951
I take its recommendations.

17
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:12,667
Then it works on its own, for almost an hour:

18
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:20,928
it lays out the push rod and the two parallelograms, picks bearings and screws for every pivot, and checks the arm at eleven poses for collisions.

19
00:01:20,928 --> 00:01:24,549
Every time the code changes, SOMA builds the model and shows it.

20
00:01:25,499 --> 00:01:26,437
Here's the arm:

21
00:01:26,437 --> 00:01:26,999
97 parts.

22
00:01:26,999 --> 00:01:31,187
The structure tree groups them into base, hardware, arm and turret.

23
00:01:31,187 --> 00:01:31,874
In the arm:

24
00:01:31,874 --> 00:01:37,562
upper arm and forearm, the elbow push rod, and two level rods with a triangle at the elbow.

25
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:40,000
That's what keeps the tool mount level.

26
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,910
Make the arm and the screws see-through, and you see the drive:

27
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:47,070
the steppers sit low on the turret, and belts turn the joints.

28
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:50,756
But does the linkage actually work?

29
00:01:50,756 --> 00:01:54,450
Let's ask for an animation of the full range of motion, with a collision check.

30
00:01:55,463 --> 00:02:02,713
It writes the motion as a series of poses, solves the linkage at every step, and lets SOMA check every tenth of a second for parts that touch.

31
00:02:03,663 --> 00:02:05,957
The animation is in the Animation tab:

32
00:02:05,957 --> 00:02:08,433
pick it, close the panel, and press play.

33
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:11,537
The turret turns 135 degrees each way.

34
00:02:11,537 --> 00:02:17,422
Then the shoulder sweeps from minus 15 to 60 degrees, and the elbow from minus 40 to plus 40.

35
00:02:17,422 --> 00:02:22,863
The push rod and the level rods follow, and the tool mount stays level the whole time.

36
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:35,238
Finally, it traces the edge of the workspace:

37
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:37,360
every corner of the allowed range.

38
00:02:39,996 --> 00:02:40,812
And the report:

39
00:02:40,812 --> 00:02:43,806
with a minimum gap of one millimeter, nothing collides.

40
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:49,956
The only contacts are the belts on their pulleys, which is intended, so the angle limits could stay as they were.

41
00:02:51,796 --> 00:02:53,799
Now a change, in words again:

42
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:58,566
more reach, 300 millimeters, and a simple gripper with a small servo.

43
00:03:01,226 --> 00:03:02,397
Again it asks first:

44
00:03:02,397 --> 00:03:08,309
where to measure the 300 millimeters, which servo, what kind of gripper, and how wide it should open.

45
00:03:08,309 --> 00:03:14,396
There's no datasheet for the servo, so it asks whether it may use typical dimensions, marked as assumed.

46
00:03:15,176 --> 00:03:21,148
Then it stretches the links, builds a gear gripper around an SG90 servo, and runs the collision check again.

47
00:03:21,148 --> 00:03:25,129
It even finds two screws that touch the gear teeth, and moves the gears.

48
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:27,877
It also warns me:

49
00:03:27,877 --> 00:03:32,909
with some combinations of arm lengths, the jaws could reach below the table at the lowest pose.

50
00:03:32,909 --> 00:03:35,346
It notes that, instead of quietly blocking it.

51
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:39,489
Now the arm reaches 300 millimeters, with a gripper at the tip.

52
00:03:42,576 --> 00:03:45,026
And the animation still works with the new geometry.

53
00:03:54,776 --> 00:03:56,561
The joint angles are parameters too.

54
00:03:56,561 --> 00:03:58,346
You find them in the Parameters tab.

55
00:03:59,046 --> 00:04:01,941
Shoulder forward, elbow down, turn the base:

56
00:04:01,941 --> 00:04:06,152
SOMA rebuilds the arm in that pose, with every rod in its place.

57
00:04:06,152 --> 00:04:07,336
No agent, no code.

58
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:11,120
Reset all brings the arm back to its home pose.

59
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:16,871
A complete linkage arm, laid out, checked and animated, just by describing it.

60
00:04:16,871 --> 00:04:18,860
Get SOMA for free at somacad.app.
